Michael Knight is Memorial has seen its  where since his death October 22nd 2011  in the city of Downey California NIDA  was a 31 year old father of four who  worked in construction on the day he  died he was out on a date with his wife  when she stopped to refill the car with  gas night I ran across the street to buy  cigarettes in the same part of town two  officers from the Downey Police  Department were investigating an armed  robbery  they approached and then detained NIDA  NIDA had nothing to do with the robbery  but grew up in South Los Angeles and had  a fear of law enforcement so after being  detained he ran from police he was  detained again shortly thereafter but  managed to escape a second time the last  time he got away sheriff officials said  that he stopped and turned around in an  aggressive manner the moment before he  turned around officer Steve Gilley fired  at night out with his gun striking him  twice in the back from 20 feet away  officers found out later that Nyjah was  unarmed night Asgardians ever since he  was a baby or Jean and Jerry Thaxton and  both refer to night as their son so when  they received a call about the shooting  they rushed the scene we were there when  they put him in the ambulance we knew he  didn't have much of a chance NIDA died  at the hospital of multiple gunshot  wounds his story may sound like a police  misconduct story you've heard before a  man was mistakenly shot unarmed and  later died except for a curious detail I  know wasn't shot with a handgun he was  shot with an mp5 submachine gun the same  gun used by the Navy SEALs why would  they have a machine gun we're not in a  war zone I didn't think I didn't think  this was a war zone why were the police  with this military type weapon it  doesn't seem to be a good answer for  that  Timothy Lynch is the director of the  project on criminal justice at the Cato  Institute he has cautioned for years  about the militarization of local police  departments your ordinary patrolman is  not going to be have on his person a  submachine gun even if they have those  types of weapons they should only be  called out in extraordinary and rare  circumstances such as when they know  that they're going to be confronting  a barricaded suspect there were a group  of suspects that they know are going to  be heavily armed Lynch says police have  been arming themselves for decades a lot  of people think the militarization of  our police departments began with 9/11  it actually started well before then in  the times where Congress wanted to ramp  up the drug war today's headlines make  clear that the war on drugs that  President Nixon declared in 1973 is  still being waged and that it's special  agents over 2,200 of them are on the  frontlines they began to make this type  of equipment available to civilian  police departments m16s grenade  launchers these armored personnel  carriers and the mp5 but it doesn't  matter what the weapon is when police  have equipment they want to use it at  first when they got it the idea was okay  well yeah this is extraordinary weaponry  we'll have it just in case we ever need  it but then as it crept along the  rationale became look we've got this  weaponry these tactics we've been doing  this military-type training let's put it  to use let's use them to execute drug  warrants first it was just our major  metropolitan areas that would have a  SWAT unit the SWAT teams are no longer  just in the big cities they're in mid  size and even small towns now and then  suddenly they're just carrying these  things in their police cars and more  routine calls the Downey Police  Department did not want to speak on  camera but told us that they acquired  the mp5s during the mid-90s that being  said it's actually hard to pin down  where police departments get machineguns  and submachine guns  sometimes they outright buy them  sometimes the money is acquired through  asset forfeiture but sometimes they get  them from someplace else they're getting  them from the Pentagon from time to time  the Pentagon makes surplus military  equipment available to the local Police  Department sometimes they're practically  just giving it away just for the  departments that will apply for it they  apply for it through the Pentagon's 1033  program a program that for 17 years has  supplied local police with millions of  dollars worth of military-grade  equipment at bottom dollar prices does  your Police Department needle  large sport utility vehicle they've got  that how about an airplane or even a  helicopter they've got those too in fact  the program grew so popular that had to  be put on hold recently when police  chiefs began transferring equipment  received elsewhere we've had some  reports of police chiefs initially  getting equipment but then maybe two  years later they are selling it to  somebody else  but it doesn't stop there politicians  have even made it easier for local  police to militarize under this program  Congress feat years ago created like a  1-800 number where the local police  departments can call find out what's  available get the application process  started  Lynch is also quick to point out that  just because police have equipment  doesn't mean they know how to use it  they've gotten their hands on the  military equipment then it gets to the  question of training and sometimes  they're not trained to use the weapons  in an appropriate fashion and sometimes  that training comes alongside members of  the military something Lynch says is  troubling because although they may look  the same their roles are very different  they both carry weapons they both wear  uniforms but that's where the  similarities end military's mission is  to find the enemy and inflict heavy  damage on them they're not thinking  about constitutional rights the civilian  police are encountering people in the  community people who do have  constitutional rights and their job is  to use the absolute minimum amount of  force so when you have these two units  training alongside one another it's  bringing the wrong type of tactics into  our communities it's totally  inappropriate versus free society it's  not just about the weapons in the  officers hands it's about the  militarization mentality local police  officers bring to their jobs our son was  shot in the back he was murdered  kidding have a weapon he wasn't involved  in a crime he wasn't on probation of  parole he was a gang member and he  wasn't safe he wasn't safe from the  people that's supposed to be protecting  him every Saturday the Thaksin's rally  with family members and friends at the  sight of knidos death and they're  looking after their grandkids even more  now he was a  man that shouldn't happen he was my baby  when he didn't feel good he always came  to me officer Gilley who fired the mp5  was put on leave but his back now on the  force temporarily reassigned to the  administrative division of the Downey  Police Department I think he needs to  make peace with his god but I really do  hope that he goes to prison for this for  killing her son
 

